Q: How to refactor multiple if-elif statements into a Strategy pattern in Python? A: <p>At some point you have to make a selection. There is no way around that. But how about this:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>FORMAT_REGISTRY = {}

class JsonProcessor:
    def process(data):
        return f&quot;Parsing JSON: {data}&quot;

FORMAT_REGISTRY['json'] = JsonProcessor

class XmlProcessor:
    def process(data):
        return f&quot;Parsing XML: {data}&quot;

FORMAT_REGISTRY['xml'] = XmlProcessor

class CsvProcessor:
    def process(data):
        return f&quot;Parsing CSV: {data}&quot;

FORMAT_REGISTRY['csv'] = CsvProcessor

# Usage
# 1. Get class by type
try:
    processor_cls = FORMAT_REGISTRY['json']
except KeyError:
    raise ValueError(&quot;Unsupported format&quot;)
# 2. Instantiate processor. Shared set of constructor arguments go here
processor = processor_cls()
# 3. process data
print(processor.process(&quot;{'key': 'value'}&quot;))
</code></pre>
<p>If a global registry does not fit your use case, you can add a factory class. But consider whether the complication is worth it over a simple function.</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>class ProcessorFactory:
    def __init__(self):
        self.format_registry = {}
        self.add_type('json', JsonProcessor)
        self.add_type('xml', XmlProcessor)
        self.add_type('csv', CsvProcessor)

    def add_type(self, label, factory_functor):
        self.format_registry[label] = factory_functor

    def make_processor(self, label):
        try:
            factory_functor = self.format_registry[label]
        except KeyError:
            raise ValueError(&quot;Unsupported format&quot;)
        return factory_functor()
</code></pre>

Q: react-table: change background color of rows depending upon row props value A: <p>First, you are wrong with assigning style. you might want to pass background-color as object style.</p>
<p>e.g.</p>
<pre><code>        return {
            style: {
                'background': rowInfo.original.customercomplaints.order_ref === currentOrderId ? '' : 'yellow',
            }
        }
</code></pre>
<p>Also instead of passing empty string as default, you might want to pass 'white' or color you want as default.</p>
